There are a few issues with your code.

1. findOne() does not return a cursor

This is incorrect:

const accounts = await collection
	.findOne({ email: req.params.email })
	.toArray();

findOne() returns a single object (or null), so remove .toArray().

2. You're checking the wrong variable

You wrote:

if (!account) {

but your variable is called accounts.

3. Correct version
app.get("/account/:email", async (req, res) => {
	try {
		await client.connect();

		const collection = client.db("allAcounts").collection("acounts");

		const account = await collection.findOne({
			email: req.params.email,
		});

		if (!account) {
			return res.status(404).send({
				error: "Account not found",
			});
		}

		res.status(200).send(account);
	} catch (error) {
		console.log(error);

		res.status(500).send({
			error: "Could not get account",
			value: error,
		});
	}
});
Postman example

If your account in MongoDB looks like this:

{
  "_id": "...",
  "name": "John",
  "email": "john@gmail.com"
}

Then in Postman:

Method

GET

URL

http://localhost:3000/account/john@gmail.com

or if your backend is on Render:

https://your-app.onrender.com/account/john@gmail.com
If the email contains @

Sometimes the @ and other special characters should be URL encoded.

For example:

john@gmail.com

becomes

john%40gmail.com

So the URL would be:

https://your-app.onrender.com/account/john%40gmail.com

Most browsers and Postman handle this automatically, but it's worth trying if you're getting unexpected results.

If you're getting 404 Not Found

There are two different kinds of 404:

1. Express route not found

The response might say something like:

Cannot GET /account/john@gmail.com

This means your route isn't being reached. Check:

Is your server running?
Did you redeploy Render?
Are you using the correct URL?
Is the route actually /account/:email?
2. Your own 404

If the response is:

{
  "error": "Account not found"
}

then the route is working, but MongoDB didn't find a document with that email.

