I think I see the problem.

Your route is:

app.get("/account/:email", ...)

but you're calling:

https://easy-animals.onrender.com/account?email=my.goodmail@email.com

Those are not the same. req.params.email only works when the email is part of the path, not the query string. Query strings are accessed through req.query.

Option 1 (keep your current route)

If your route is:

app.get("/account/:email", ...)

then the URL should be:

https://easy-animals.onrender.com/account/my.goodmail@email.com

or, even better, URL-encoded:

https://easy-animals.onrender.com/account/my.goodmail%40email.com
Option 2 (use the URL you're already calling)

If you want to keep using:

https://easy-animals.onrender.com/account?email=my.goodmail@email.com

then change your route to use req.query instead:

app.get("/account", async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const collection = client.db("allAcounts").collection("acounts");

        const account = await collection.findOne({
            email: req.query.email,
        });

        if (!account) {
            return res.status(404).send({
                error: "Account not found",
            });
        }

        res.send(account);
    } catch (error) {
        res.status(500).send({
            error: "Could not get account",
            value: error,
        });
    }
});

This route matches the URL you're sending.

One more thing: you said the response is "Cannot GET /account".

That suggests your deployed server may not actually have a GET /account route. If your code is using:

app.get("/account/:email", ...)

then requesting /account?email=... should still match /account with a query string if the route is defined as /account, but not if it's only /account/:email. The "Cannot GET" message usually means the requested path doesn't match any route.

